---
import '../../styles/development-editorial.css';
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import LegacyHeader from "../../components/LegacyHeader.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../../components/Hero.astro";
import ButtonPrimary from "../../components/ButtonPrimary.astro";
import ButtonSecondary from "../../components/ButtonSecondary.astro";
import DevelopmentStackJobs from "../../components/DevelopmentStackJobs.astro";
const canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/development/nextjs/";
const proof=[
  {
    "title": "Rendering chosen per route",
    "text": "Built once, refreshed after an edit or rendered per request. A public page gets its content into the HTML. A signed-in dashboard does not need to."
  },
  {
    "title": "Publishing path agreed",
    "text": "A CMS edit reaches the public page through a rebuild or revalidation, depending on the deployment. We agree the delay, then test it."
  },
  {
    "title": "Host matched to features",
    "text": "Draft Mode, on-demand revalidation and Server Actions need a compatible server deployment. A static export supports none of them."
  }
];
const checks=[
  {
    "title": "Crawlable pages",
    "text": "Search engines need clean URLs, internal links, server-rendered content where needed and sensible indexation controls."
  },
  {
    "title": "Content structure",
    "text": "Pages need headings, body copy, proof, FAQs and conversion points that support SEO rather than sit behind visual components."
  },
  {
    "title": "Performance and Core Web Vitals",
    "text": "We reduce bloat, unnecessary scripts, layout shifts and render delays that weaken user experience."
  },
  {
    "title": "Redirect and migration safety",
    "text": "When replacing an old site, URL mapping and launch QA matter more than a prettier homepage."
  },
  {
    "title": "Schema and metadata",
    "text": "Titles, descriptions, canonical tags, structured data and Open Graph basics should be built in, not bolted on later."
  },
  {
    "title": "Analytics and forms",
    "text": "A site that cannot track enquiries properly makes SEO reporting weaker than it needs to be."
  }
];
const process=[
  {
    "title": "Audit",
    "text": "Review the current site, rankings, crawl behaviour, content, analytics and conversion risks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Plan",
    "text": "Map templates, URLs, content sections, redirects, schema and development priorities."
  },
  {
    "title": "Build",
    "text": "Develop the site around speed, accessibility, crawlability and editorial control."
  },
  {
    "title": "QA",
    "text": "Test pages, metadata, forms, mobile layouts, redirects, schema and indexation settings."
  },
  {
    "title": "Launch",
    "text": "Deploy with a launch checklist, Search Console monitoring and post-launch crawl checks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Improve",
    "text": "Use ranking, crawl and conversion data to refine pages after launch."
  }
];
const faqs=[
  {
    "question": "Does a Next.js site need a server?",
    "answer": "Not always. A static export can be served as plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript from almost any host. Features such as runtime Draft Mode, on-demand revalidation and Server Actions need a compatible server deployment. We list the features the site needs and check them against the proposed host before choosing the deployment model, because changing it later means rework."
  },
  {
    "question": "How will a CMS edit reach the public page?",
    "answer": "That depends on the rendering and cache configuration. A static export needs a new build; a server deployment may use revalidation after publication. We agree the expected publishing delay and check an edit on the deployed page, including its title and canonical. We also test a deleted entry, since a cache can keep serving a page the CMS no longer holds. Cache behaviour must be checked against the installed Next.js version and configuration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should logged-in pages appear in Google?",
    "answer": "No. Private account content should stay behind authenticated access, and public product and service pages need their own accessible content and links. A loading state inside an account is a different problem from missing content on a public landing page. A noindex tag is also no substitute for access control: it asks search engines to stay away and does nothing to stop a signed-out visitor."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can editors preview unpublished content?",
    "answer": "Yes, with work. Next.js Draft Mode can support a CMS preview, but it needs integration and a server deployment. The scope should define who can enter preview, how draft access is protected and how editors return to the published version. Installing Next.js does not include any of it."
  },
  {
    "question": "What do you need before quoting a rebuild?",
    "answer": "Share the repository or current website, the Next.js version, hosting setup, CMS, public routes and any authentication or API dependencies. We agree who approves releases and who owns dependency updates, hosting accounts and rollback. Ongoing maintenance and backend changes need an explicit scope, set out in the written proposal."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can you work alongside our developer, and will rankings improve?",
    "answer": "Yes to the first. We can provide SEO direction, implementation guidance and launch QA alongside your developer. On the second, redirects, content, metadata, links and indexation are all checked during a rebuild, but no framework and no launch checklist guarantees rankings."
  }
];
---
<MainLayout title="Next.js Development Philippines | Bright Forge" description="Next.js development from the Philippines: rendering, caching and hosting decided route by route, so public pages rank and the build stays maintainable." canonical={canonical} type="service" breadcrumbs={[{name:"Home",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/"},{name:"Development",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/development/"},{name:"Next.js",url:canonical}]} faqs={faqs}>
<LegacyHeader slot="header" />
<Hero title="Next.js <span>Development</span>" breadcrumbCurrent="Next.js">
<Fragment slot="story"><p class="hero-subtitle">Next.js development for websites that mix public pages, CMS content and signed-in features, with rendering decided route by route.</p><p>Next.js lets every route be built once, refreshed after an edit, rendered per request or left to the browser. That freedom is its strength and its SEO risk. We decide how each route is rendered, how editors publish and what the host must run, so product pages are discoverable and private account details are not. Those decisions are settled before interface work starts.</p><div class="hero-proof">{proof.map((item)=>(<article><strong>{item.title}</strong><span>{item.text}</span></article>))}</div></Fragment>
<Fragment slot="actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="#checks" text="See What We Check" /></Fragment>
</Hero>
<main class="dev-page">
<section id="checks"><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Build focus</p><h2>Next.js for SEO: <span>rendering, caching and metadata</span> settled for every public route</h2><p>A product site may have a public pricing page, CMS-managed guides and a signed-in dashboard. They do not need the same caching or access rules. We map those requirements alongside the existing URLs, metadata and internal links.</p><p>When a Next.js site loses search visibility, the framework is rarely at fault. The cause is usually one of three build decisions. A public page fetches its main content in the browser, so the HTML a crawler receives holds only a loading state. A cached page keeps serving a product the CMS deleted last week. A dynamic route ships without its own title and canonical. Each is cheap to prevent at the planning stage and slow to find after launch.</p><p>Illustrative acceptance check: we publish an approved CMS edit, then inspect the public page outside preview. The changed copy and metadata should appear within the agreed publishing window, while a signed-out visitor still cannot retrieve private account data. Checks like this are written into the scope before the build starts.</p><p>The nearest build you can inspect is <a href="/development/#workbench">Shinobi</a>, our own brand site for creative businesses: a React build on TanStack Start, alongside this site, which runs on Astro. The Shinobi launch sheet is published on our development page, with routes, titles, redirects and the share card each checked on the live host, and a Next.js build is held to the same standard.</p></div><aside class="panel"><h3>What we check first</h3><p>Which routes are public, how often their data changes, whether editors need preview and which server features the host supports. We also review forms, analytics and the old-to-new URL map.</p></aside></div></section>
<DevelopmentStackJobs
  title="Four decisions we settle on every <span>Next.js</span> build"
  intro="Each one affects hosting cost, content freshness and what your team must maintain after handover."
  jobs={[
    {title:"Rendering for each route", text:"We choose build-time, revalidated or request-time rendering around how often the content changes. We read the returned HTML of each public page for its copy and links, separately from authenticated screens."},
    {title:"Publishing and cache refresh", text:"We record what triggers revalidation or a rebuild, and test changed and deleted CMS content as well as the first successful page load, because a stale cache is invisible until a customer finds it."},
    {title:"Metadata, preview and production", text:"We keep draft access separate from public output and verify that every deployed route, dynamic ones included, carries its own title, canonical, Open Graph tags and schema."},
    {title:"Release, upgrades and rollback", text:"We confirm runtime support, environment settings and the release owner, test redirects, plan version upgrades as their own piece of work and keep a route back to the previous deployment."}
  ]}
/>
<section><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who this suits</p><h2>Where <span>Next.js earns its maintenance cost</span>, and where a simpler stack wins</h2><p>Next.js suits SaaS sites, portals, dashboards and marketing sites with live data: products where public pages, CMS content and signed-in features share one React codebase and must stay crawlable and fast. The price is an application to look after. There is a server runtime to host, a cache to reason about, and major versions that have changed routing and caching behaviour, so upgrades need a developer and a test plan.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>Before we recommend Next.js</h3><p>If editors mainly publish service pages and articles, Astro with a CMS or WordPress may meet the brief with fewer application dependencies. Next.js earns its place when the route, data and interface requirements justify maintaining that application layer.</p></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><p class="eyebrow">Related development services</p><h2>Compare Next.js with <span>simpler ways to publish</span></h2><div class="link-grid"><a href="/development/astro/"><span>Astro development</span>Content-led sites shipped as HTML. What this site runs on.</a><a href="/development/wordpress/"><span>WordPress development</span>Editors publish without a deploy, in return for regular updates.</a><a href="/development/nextjs/"><span>Next.js development</span>One React codebase for public pages, live data and accounts.</a></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container faq-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p><h2><span>Next.js development</span> questions on hosting, publishing and preview</h2></div><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map((faq)=>(<details class="faq-item"><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>))}</div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><div class="cta-card"><h2>Planning a Next.js build? Settle <span>rendering and hosting</span> first.</h2><p>Send the repository or current site, the routes that must rank and the features that need a server. We will review them and say whether Next.js fits, and what the architecture should be, before anyone builds the interface.</p><div class="cta-actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/" text="Technical SEO" /></div></div></div></section>
</main><Footer slot="footer"/>
</MainLayout>

